Test that the asn1c compiler produces compilable code for basic ASN.1 types,
while trying to disable different codecs and vary other compiler flags.

The basic type smoke tests build each generated converter with one make job.
Automake can still run separate tests concurrently, so use a bounded outer job
count to limit resource use, for example `make -j2 check`. To investigate the
three tests affected by the 2026-11-02 Intel macOS report, run:

    make -j1 check TESTS='check-SEQUENCE_f_INTEGER.sh check-UTF8String.sh check-REAL.sh'

The reported compiler processes received SIGKILL; the log does not identify
what sent the signals. Limiting parallelism may reduce resource pressure but
cannot prevent an external service from terminating a process. If the failures
continue, check the host's security and system logs around the failing run.

`check-CER.sh` generates real C descriptors and verifies CER encoder and
decoder behavior for primitive values, constructed strings, tagged values,
sets, choices, and selected open types. It also checks how CER interacts with
disabled codecs and compiler flags.

`check-issue-588.sh` compiles generated code for a parameterized object-set
alias whose courtesy includes form a cycle through a CHOICE. It checks both
ASN.1 module orders with dependency includes enabled and disabled. The alias
declaration must be available before the header follows courtesy includes.
With dependency includes enabled, the test also checks both alias declarations
precede the include and compiles each header as an independent C translation
unit. It checks C++ compilation too when a C++ compiler is installed.

`check-issue-587.sh` compiles two parameterized object-set specializations
inside a `SEQUENCE OF` in both ASN.1 module orders. It checks the generated
member constraint callbacks and open-type descriptors and then compiles the
generated C, catching duplicate internal type suffixes.

`check-issue-607.sh` covers GitHub issue #594 using fixture number 607. It
builds generated descriptors in native and wide storage modes. It checks absent
INTEGER and BOOLEAN DEFAULTs in SET, absent REAL DEFAULT
in SEQUENCE, exact pretty/canonical XER output, minified JER REAL DEFAULT output,
and that encoding leaves the source structure unchanged.

Run it from the smoke-test directory with `make check TESTS=check-CER.sh`.
Run the order-sensitive object-set regressions with
`make check TESTS='check-issue-587.sh check-issue-588.sh'`, and the generated XER
regression with `make check TESTS=check-issue-607.sh`.
The script uses Bash, the configured C compiler and make, and standard tools
such as `awk`, `cmp`, and `mktemp`. Its expected long BIT STRING vector writes
payload octets with Bash's explicit `printf '\377'` escape so byte values do
not depend on `awk` character conversion or the active locale.

The locale-sensitive expected-vector issue found during the 2026-10-13
investigation is recorded in [docs/cer-encoder-plan.md](../../docs/cer-encoder-plan.md).
